# writing.zd — the `static` placement, running.
#
# The milestone-7 target is a `client` + `static` application (§14C.3b):
# content fixed at build time, plus whatever the reader is doing right
# now. This file is the smallest program that is one.
#
# `posts` is `static`, so it is computed once by `zdc build`, on the build
# host, and **inlined into the bundle as a literal**. Read from the view it
# is `List of Post` and not `Remote of List of Post`, because no boundary
# is crossed — §5.2's Rule 1 is satisfied here rather than excepted
# (§14G.1.4).
#
# The consequences are visible in `dist/`: `client.js` contains the titles
# as JavaScript literals, emits no `$remote` binding for them, and `dist/`
# has no `functions/` directory at all. There is no request to make.

record Post
    slug is Text
    # `Markup`, not `Text`. `build markdown` is the only expression in the
    # language that produces one, and `Prose` is the only element that
    # renders one as HTML. Neither converts to the other in either
    # direction, so the field's type is what makes the body renderable and
    # the slug not.
    body is Markup

# The milestone-7 case, running. `content/` is three real markdown files
# in this repository; this line is what turns them into a `List of Post`
# in the bundle.
#
# `readPosts` reaches the filesystem through **compiler-provided build
# capabilities** — `build list`, `build read`, `build markdown` — and not
# through an imported module. The distinction is not stylistic. A *runtime*
# `foreign` (§14E) calls into a real host: a browser or a serverless
# runtime, which genuinely has npm and a DOM. A *build-time* call has no
# host. The compiler is the host. §17.4.8's claim that build-time `foreign`
# works "because the build host can import `marked`" was true only under
# the `node` approach §7 forbids, and the build host is in fact the
# compiler's own engine, which ships ECMAScript builtins and nothing else.
#
# What the capability form buys, and a module loader could not:
#
#   * `zdc` stays one binary — nothing is fetched, resolved, or installed.
#   * It is sandboxed. Every path is resolved and then required to be
#     inside this directory, so `..`, an absolute path, and a symbolic
#     link out of the project are each a build error.
#   * It is deterministic. `build list` sorts, so the same three files
#     inline in the same order on every machine.
#
# And the cost, stated rather than argued away: **the set is closed**, and
# grows only with the compiler. What bounds that is that growing it spends
# no keyword — `read`, `list` and `markdown` are identifiers inside the
# `build` production, so a fourth capability reserves nothing.
state posts is static List of Post from readPosts with directory is "content"

function readPosts with directory
    from build list directory
    map each path to postFrom with path

# `slug` is the path, because deriving one from the file name needs text
# operations and §14F has no standard library to take them from. That is a
# missing library, not a missing capability — the same shape as the note
# on `feed` below.
function postFrom with path
    give Post with slug is path, body is build markdown (build read path)

# Derived at build time from build-time data: still `static`, still free.
# The whole derivation runs on the build host and only its result ships.
state heading is static Text from titleFor with posts

function titleFor with all
    give "Writing"

# §14C.3b's other half: `static` must be able to *emit* files, not only
# read them. The portfolio's `rss.xml` and `llms.txt` are build-time
# outputs derived from build-time inputs, and they are generated *files*
# rather than endpoints — both of the target's two `route.ts` files are
# `force-static`.
#
# Deriving them from the same state the pages are built from is what keeps
# them from drifting: there is no second copy of the content to forget to
# update, because `feed` and the view read the one `posts`.
# The feed is derived from `heading`, which is derived from `posts` — the
# same state the view renders. One source, so nothing can drift.
#
# It is not yet derived from every post's title, and the reason is §14F:
# folding a `List of Post` into one piece of `Text` needs `join`, and there
# is no standard library to take it from. That is a missing library, not a
# missing placement.
state feed is static Text from feedFor with heading emitting "rss.xml"

function feedFor with title
    give "<rss version='2.0'><channel><title>" + title + "</title></channel></rss>"

# What the reader is doing. `client`, because it is theirs and it is gone
# on reload — which is correct.
state query is client Text starting ""

view
    Column
        Heading heading
        Input query, hint is "filter posts"

        each post in posts
            Row
                Text post.slug
                # `Prose` parses its argument as HTML. It is the only
                # element that does, and it takes `Markup` and nothing
                # else — writing `Prose post.slug` is a compile error, and
                # so is `Text post.body`.
                Prose post.body
